#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "260712d9-b48d-552e-a31a-0c2e74cafeb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6241" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/4f/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/0a/0ad490af48e1db93ab9cae7e482b5eb8956aa14f8d2972816f8852179b35c4cb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/a3/a33927647d7651ce47c9e6619bd26a29ceb5cc5dd74e379bf81ea61806fb5c95.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=2+3+4+5=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "07269d0a-faf3-5bf9-b397-8fc1574313a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6242" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3b/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/59/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=15 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=10 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/id #uuid "60ec2df0-87b9-544f-b286-2ed5e17d92fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9476" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbb0a6a-df5c-5bc1-8a49-a4c4fdf2a5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540604} {:answer/id #uuid "35cdbd40-9a06-5e92-a857-4869a4c940eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}-{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540612} {:answer/id #uuid "47e58e88-a133-5e35-b49f-24dd21975b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{21}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540655} {:answer/id #uuid "915ebf17-94f8-5c72-ae44-676a73d72d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540771} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc5a8e2-26fa-5e37-961f-3fa132a8b42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fc5a8e2-26fa-5e37-961f-3fa132a8b42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540795} :answer-field/id #uuid "1006f306-981a-511e-a016-496ffa5b9202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "688728a4-696e-5eca-909c-9cdebd1b8f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122812" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee1a59d15c7ad98ef2f0dd79d622c74f9fd419fa24db4212fe3bb35030e806a8.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\cdot {m}_{11}+2\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12704151-2a3f-5c78-82a6-3698cf12fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540559} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4984a1-bf54-50db-b28e-4424d035c287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540581} {:answer/id #uuid "81e1aec9-02c9-5d64-aa88-79ce6c1d9935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}-{m}_{21}-{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540746} {:answer/id #uuid "b62b1dcc-a7a2-515a-82fc-67a62e3f0afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}-{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540832} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0995e6-6731-5004-bd04-b2911f5c2cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{21}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4984a1-bf54-50db-b28e-4424d035c287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540581} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee86ad7-df28-501c-a81c-6bd872b01f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f89ca459-c179-53fe-83c0-45ad6670695a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122874" :question/problem "![](images/03/03935d4a02a1a42a106e148fa120132a246818684f88a441f63de46bc01dcbdc.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+1\\cdot {m}_{21}+1\\cdot {m}_{12}+1\\cdot {m}_{22} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f5eef9-7737-53db-9ca6-2b4f8ce6ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254844} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5e4304-b99f-5bd7-93cf-6e94f4c86382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255469} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a50015-07a5-503c-8130-1145ba038fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255686} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8b3d98-9e1a-5e4e-bc4a-f79737ae2797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255877} {:answer/id #uuid "fb92baf3-6316-5fdb-b6dc-e4f11a432f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb92baf3-6316-5fdb-b6dc-e4f11a432f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255878} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7b8ffb-61aa-5f48-af1c-7a2746cabdc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f64134f-4f5d-5d15-bd1f-7a73860394f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122876" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0abae6be42361d157d7cbd46f602d3a0d2044cfec551c61a2739ff9b56798cc3.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{12}+3\\cdot {m}_{13} \\\\  & =3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d188a23-dab3-5b6e-8263-d8a3e3585db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540552} {:answer/id #uuid "8937b8f1-b84c-52e8-97eb-b8be5b9c9cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+3{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540760} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7801d-9b07-56e2-a485-354d609cc6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540845} {:answer/id #uuid "c84f6a96-35e9-5f82-ba6e-69dc607bc73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540863} {:answer/id #uuid "d8551ee9-261d-5834-b7b2-72906db68e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d188a23-dab3-5b6e-8263-d8a3e3585db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540552} :answer-field/id #uuid "366ce63e-4758-55ab-ade5-2bbdeee37bb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfbdcb48-ce24-5aa4-b673-ed006db801e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122885" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b00e13fc7b827b9c3960073679ee651f6bce5a6193e0b0af1907738691a287ed.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=1,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+2\\cdot {m}_{12} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d6fd7a-77ba-50da-9b48-5f42b4a3f71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540529} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1831a8-7319-5cf3-9494-86fd997692f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540545} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdf7f22-f9ed-5577-a8be-e560b2430cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540627} {:answer/id #uuid "9f49662e-9f1d-5dac-a394-6f82ad2e70e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540794} {:answer/id #uuid "dabe35cb-066f-51f0-aee7-336adb36649f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bdf7f22-f9ed-5577-a8be-e560b2430cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540627} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb531dc-a984-5ad5-8e3d-3ead33a40597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde06bdc-b497-5379-91e5-90582a7fb14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122886" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9544b511212f20620d516a6f14fcfa1d812a0119cc14f8741d113c6cb663961.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+1\\cdot {m}_{21}+2\\cdot {m}_{12}+2\\cdot {m}_{22} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0fa39e0-956a-5971-a717-a2b4ddbbbc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148908} {:answer/id #uuid "d0077eb1-34bb-5600-b1a2-be8c9f158d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148909} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c95708-1325-5ab4-8b83-59715ff43a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148910} {:answer/id #uuid "3553af4c-5978-53c6-badb-cb42ce8cf148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148911} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaedb76-9489-527b-ac20-15c077ad82b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0fa39e0-956a-5971-a717-a2b4ddbbbc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148908} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af36f43-964d-53de-b148-316a8c5425f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fa3d711-e06b-53b5-b006-e7a59f8870a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122960" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=6-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/17/178108b256e55eaa5bd58c72f606fbed0aef774ff10a46e059c70abe93f8b341.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/dd/dd521e876abfc4313afb5beaf568c1d93cad670cfa912e210ed5d1548c51ec97.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/05/0515af2764a6ac27b2b69998ea4b99079dd7266a5989a161d01a6d25a670678e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d1d7b0-c3fd-5485-8422-18c8e702a67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253718} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b8fb4-f023-56a1-85b9-e4ae5bccefda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254055} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ad179e-3d69-5623-85a0-28650c81d69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255288} {:answer/id #uuid "be9a04f6-9dee-58fc-8b94-1ff8129251db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255372} {:answer/id #uuid "d32a4f4a-ab08-51b8-a6bb-fb899792fed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32a4f4a-ab08-51b8-a6bb-fb899792fed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255554} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc40bd39-8703-5c2c-bf5c-0285eba26a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee2cf7dd-c375-54b8-8b2a-262bd6f0e8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122961" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-x-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/48/48356d566752c064e9381c2f9ce27ef12bbdd86a29a2dab0d3dda05088630006.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})+{m}_{41}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{41})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}+2{m}_{41}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-x-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/90/90ed44b97d085413780f2dce297dd78ffa4c968a244e73c431d2e9aa30e14d22.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/86/860ba8cf7e5135f7e2eedcde7534fe9880feb38c29163625e32b79f18fce2f2f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,3)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,3)=1 \\\\ {m}_{41} & =f(4,3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf9001c-70ea-56d0-96ab-7510337b0d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254198} {:answer/id #uuid "6c398f6a-a289-5337-a54e-a7de1833b166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254632} {:answer/id #uuid "a446fe5e-a864-5311-8722-0a827afcb605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255117} {:answer/id #uuid "d521f2cc-aef2-57a8-8960-63eeeeb3f75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255562} {:answer/id #uuid "d8606b92-4138-5bac-a6bf-6662677d8bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a446fe5e-a864-5311-8722-0a827afcb605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255117} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7d53ae-c503-53b3-aa1d-83173cc4b5a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47dab4f9-83eb-55b5-936b-f3f44a3e6af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122965" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-2x-2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/46/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-2x-2y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-2x-2y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/52/52c19da39a43cdbf1b1d2565669c391e59679b3bf9828f7c46755831b2b0890c.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/2a/2acddf617eda8211e45d281329051f08a8b83788e24b70ebe8d526b58b195bba.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,3)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=5+3+3+1=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3f6271-26ac-5779-9fc0-4fb7ad90aa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540622} {:answer/id #uuid "688c2410-3624-5fc3-b9cb-d5377915e7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540705} {:answer/id #uuid "6ade1e9f-48c8-5454-bff2-0de3aabc833c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540711} {:answer/id #uuid "888bac61-eb50-50bf-adde-8d2e2ddef8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540756} {:answer/id #uuid "fa76ed60-6738-528c-9aac-b75fcea7735d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ade1e9f-48c8-5454-bff2-0de3aabc833c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540711} :answer-field/id #uuid "14455e9e-ecaa-5111-b90d-483eb946543b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ad4ccbd-e338-5550-8a1d-a1b82832243e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122966" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=11-{x}^{2}-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3b/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=11-{x}^{2}-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=11-{x}^{2}-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/59/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=6 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=6+1+5+0=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135cdf1a-54bd-5516-b63f-c6dd787df5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253836} {:answer/id #uuid "14592777-13ea-5aa8-9a5d-9542c0a87417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253842} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc9310f-08d7-54cb-9fe4-e39e036154cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255346} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb5c86d-bcba-5c4b-8e15-19a38ace784c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255618} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a3e07c-83c6-5375-8b22-6bd840c4d6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbc9310f-08d7-54cb-9fe4-e39e036154cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8abff1-1635-5a0e-87c3-9c866bf145ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aea78735-0849-5195-bf1a-fcbc0a64996f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122982" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/c3/c30acb7cb94886e612675d5e64838961134e8d09e5d88c1d803067832f4b173e.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/e5/e5c92768512480b16fcca735126b303c4ca372d22a5c735fc8cea1588a3c5811.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/b0/b015f94b135ca669c0711057c7245982eb8f0257e4947a603571f2826364b8fa.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=4 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396bfa62-01a9-5b70-9e03-5d0ccd21ca1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342708} {:answer/id #uuid "58efafd9-0e55-54fa-89da-75a5b14765d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342985} {:answer/id #uuid "c03eda25-28c5-55d0-9623-4a2b220286ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343923} {:answer/id #uuid "e707f7dc-1cfb-5863-be05-a5fed5e55b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344264} {:answer/id #uuid "f28887b3-64e2-594a-836f-5dbc8e3ae588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f28887b3-64e2-594a-836f-5dbc8e3ae588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344369} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0e92f3-9fc2-5d4a-b431-65a2bb867825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ff7ee9c-86e4-5538-972f-ae0d4081ca31" :question/math-academy-id "q-75035" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/46/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/19/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/a8/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107dfb62-1b9d-51c8-9744-e1f29dd07655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540558} {:answer/id #uuid "88b3a027-2fb3-5589-8df2-d6ac588524bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540757} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8d42e0-eaa9-505b-8931-064524f4bd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540817} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6dcd45-400c-5812-8da7-5f3fb8564611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540839} {:answer/id #uuid "f58f6e34-2e0f-5f5d-832d-5b3725016996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8d42e0-eaa9-505b-8931-064524f4bd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540817} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a4ee6d-5997-53cb-a03a-d8841c187de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73bd1c38-9ed6-5451-94fd-2aa984462a1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75039" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/a3/a30d43569026cdf8f4ce960016620a725248726523abd005dbad5bc903123b9f.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/a8/a81396ee6d33b1ac10a44b6b8db2ded2612e86274e017ebe95c55420b7f73265.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c9/c90a6e6d4c9e9a92bfd1c42b8350941b7d45f96d422e2a1988a4c003df6457e1.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=8 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=13 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,3)=13 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,3)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =8+13+13+18 \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0248a542-3d5b-5928-a2e6-ed3d44000240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342269} {:answer/id #uuid "68626c9c-1d01-579b-922b-3d14e2b91c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343129} {:answer/id #uuid "9f96f715-530c-544b-a88b-c2183948fa0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343647} {:answer/id #uuid "b671d714-bebb-5977-ba6b-cae7a6593160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343837} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f34c8a-9072-53da-a0fd-9123e2dabb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0248a542-3d5b-5928-a2e6-ed3d44000240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6c9384-fd49-55e2-9775-cb3e79c43490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10db6ae3-22d1-573c-bc93-e20a86099dc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75040" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3c/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/d7/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c6/c6bdb6a479878c3fed94923a5db19d5e55db3431b685344d54b9a384f70a60e3.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,0)=11 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=11 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =10+11+11+12 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1029b904-d7c1-500f-a5fc-c3fef9409d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342379} {:answer/id #uuid "241589eb-3016-56df-a377-d69cc4dcade5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342539} {:answer/id #uuid "430c6d53-8fb2-5bd6-88d1-5b83a7937f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342798} {:answer/id #uuid "91313571-1001-5065-901e-34e568033801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343510} {:answer/id #uuid "c066d033-69a3-566a-850f-10a66e17e2e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91313571-1001-5065-901e-34e568033801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343510} :answer-field/id #uuid "234979b0-dc88-5202-a5e1-7c82a702597e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84cead96-82e2-593c-b11d-991e52abf636" :question/math-academy-id "q-75230" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b26ea696afda59a7181f062c7c9fab4be373035678eafa0da51bf2b22f9f4b3.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{m}_{23}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $6$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}+{m}_{13}+{m}_{23}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/70/70e4f9cd213e0dc4429e21cacf5c375d4def1e9ba021f05e45ee6e7820c5ff36.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/81/8135011b8384a668d4bab28282220bb0560e1ae64c2f1d623822435349067f18.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(1,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{23} & =f(2,3)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2+3+3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd184c5-a1ab-5ea2-a565-dc9e11d0577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540551} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9bb78a-6144-5f51-9960-0c3b78126ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540578} {:answer/id #uuid "47051153-0c3e-51ec-b6c0-503c70ae170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540652} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a25326-be2d-5c80-8dde-5f079287a42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540813} {:answer/id #uuid "b81ae7bf-dac2-515c-8794-1cd63313830e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cd184c5-a1ab-5ea2-a565-dc9e11d0577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540551} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c51992-c8f6-553a-ab49-de91cc2fad16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68f740c4-3eaf-53af-86af-a7b05d82500b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75253" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/29/293e79685a3f4da205d986bcf4331e4b4f43c0a48fe183d27f93aaee6bb17b99.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6e04660f00d04307715159c25aa2860ca08af74ed36fc9b0be15d10ee94a27.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/74/744aeb24bb4fd4487b35ffd789d957879cb0c3ee92e40519f71b65ee5ee76dcf.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(4,2)=6 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,4)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(4,4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 6+4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=80.\n$$"}]}
